1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,375
Hello everyone, I'm
Kishore Chandra Rajapudi.

2
00:00:02,870 --> 00:00:06,802
Thank you for joining me at Conf42
Chaos Engineering twenty twenty-six.

3
00:00:07,632 --> 00:00:11,140
Imagine a customer placing an
order through a retail app.

4
00:00:11,335 --> 00:00:14,486
The payment request times out,
the order is still pending,

5
00:00:14,997 --> 00:00:16,097
and the customer tries again.

6
00:00:16,706 --> 00:00:20,689
Several services may still appear
healthy while the customer has no

7
00:00:20,716 --> 00:00:22,314
clear answer about what happened.

8
00:00:22,950 --> 00:00:27,298
Today, I'll walk through a proposed
architecture for handling situations

9
00:00:27,317 --> 00:00:31,123
like this using agentic AI and
controlled recovery workflow.

10
00:00:31,703 --> 00:00:35,262
The agent helps interpret
evidence and recommend a response.

11
00:00:35,822 --> 00:00:39,718
A separate policy layer controls
which actions are allowed.

12
00:00:40,134 --> 00:00:45,441
An au-authorized person initiates
reopening, and the system

13
00:00:45,442 --> 00:00:48,564
validates the transaction journey
before accepting new business.

14
00:00:48,923 --> 00:00:53,498
I'll cover the architecture, walk through
an example incident, and explain how I

15
00:00:53,499 --> 00:00:55,343
would test its behavior under failure.

16
00:00:55,741 --> 00:00:59,747
Let's begin with why retail
incidents need this broader view

17
00:01:00,747 --> 00:01:04,411
A retail transaction
crosses several systems.

18
00:01:05,187 --> 00:01:09,884
Ordering depends on payment, inventory,
and often loyalty and delivery.

19
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:14,184
The customer experiences
all of these as one journey.

20
00:01:14,638 --> 00:01:16,515
That creates the first challenge.

21
00:01:16,761 --> 00:01:21,336
A failure in one dependency can
prevent the business outcome even when

22
00:01:21,401 --> 00:01:23,083
other services are running normally.

23
00:01:23,708 --> 00:01:26,220
The second challenge
is fragmented evidence.

24
00:01:26,948 --> 00:01:31,471
One dashboard shows timeouts,
another shows delayed events,

25
00:01:31,551 --> 00:01:34,332
and the support ticket says
customer cannot complete checkout.

26
00:01:35,052 --> 00:01:40,089
Operations needs to connect
those observations to the

27
00:01:40,108 --> 00:01:41,711
affected transactions and stores.

28
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:44,163
The third challenge is the recovery gap.

29
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:49,963
An alert might clear because
traffic has dropped or a store

30
00:01:49,974 --> 00:01:51,430
has dropped accepting orders.

31
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:54,979
That does not demonstrate
that checkout works again.

32
00:01:55,510 --> 00:01:59,372
Our operational goal is to protect
the complete transaction journey.

33
00:01:59,623 --> 00:02:04,139
We need to understand the customer
impact, limit further exposure,

34
00:02:04,746 --> 00:02:07,786
and gather evidence that normal
business can safely resume.

35
00:02:08,418 --> 00:02:12,027
That goal shapes the definition
of self-healing on the next slide

36
00:02:13,027 --> 00:02:14,827
What self-healing should mean.

37
00:02:15,377 --> 00:02:18,232
In this design, self-healing
has four responsibilities.

38
00:02:18,478 --> 00:02:20,706
First, detect and interpret.

39
00:02:21,074 --> 00:02:26,211
We connect transaction outcomes
with service and dependency

40
00:02:26,211 --> 00:02:32,036
signals, so we can describe what
is failing and who is affected.

41
00:02:32,391 --> 00:02:34,758
Second, contain safely.

42
00:02:35,364 --> 00:02:39,749
Deterministic rules decide whether a
specific protective action is permitted.

43
00:02:40,044 --> 00:02:45,163
For example, an affected store might
temporarily stop accepting new digital

44
00:02:45,183 --> 00:02:46,914
orders while the team investigates.

45
00:02:47,605 --> 00:02:49,433
Third, recover with ownership.

46
00:02:49,495 --> 00:02:52,997
An authorized operator reviews
the situation and initiates

47
00:02:53,022 --> 00:02:54,581
the reopening process.

48
00:02:54,998 --> 00:02:57,933
That makes responsibility for
the recovery decision explicit.

49
00:02:58,352 --> 00:03:00,181
Fourth, verify outcomes.

50
00:03:01,212 --> 00:03:05,384
The workflow checks dependency path,
the transaction path, and relevant

51
00:03:05,387 --> 00:03:07,759
business state before the store reopens.

52
00:03:08,387 --> 00:03:11,821
These responsibilities define
the limits of automation.

53
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:15,171
The system can collect
evidence, coordinate work, and

54
00:03:15,171 --> 00:03:16,772
perform approved containment.

55
00:03:17,145 --> 00:03:20,982
Reopening follow human initiation
and successful validation.

56
00:03:21,256 --> 00:03:24,568
Now, let's map those responsibilities
onto the architecture

57
00:03:25,568 --> 00:03:28,568
The proposed autonomous
retail operations agent.

58
00:03:28,832 --> 00:03:33,470
Starting on the left, retail
services produce signals about

59
00:03:33,497 --> 00:03:35,358
orders, payments, and inventory.

60
00:03:35,582 --> 00:03:40,390
The telemetry and events layer brings
together transaction outcomes and

61
00:03:40,391 --> 00:03:45,059
dependency behavior with enough context to
identify the affected store and incident.

62
00:03:45,521 --> 00:03:48,881
The agent uses that evidence to
develop a possible explanation

63
00:03:49,146 --> 00:03:50,259
and suggest a response.

64
00:03:50,633 --> 00:03:56,511
Its output should include the observations
behind the recommendation and uncertainty.

65
00:03:57,117 --> 00:03:59,963
That recommendation then passes
to the policy engine that is

66
00:03:59,968 --> 00:04:02,374
separate enforcement component.

67
00:04:02,850 --> 00:04:07,949
It checks permissions, the allowed scope,
and the conditions required for an action.

68
00:04:08,578 --> 00:04:13,883
The operations workflow carries out the
approved steps such as disabling new order

69
00:04:13,885 --> 00:04:18,724
acceptance, notifying the pos- responsible
team, and coordinating validation.

70
00:04:19,083 --> 00:04:22,016
Underneath these components
is a durable incident record.

71
00:04:22,297 --> 00:04:28,758
It preserves the evidence, decisions and
actions, outcomes, so the workflow can be

72
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:30,902
reviewed and resumed after interruption.

73
00:04:31,643 --> 00:04:35,027
The word autonomous here refers
to the work the system can

74
00:04:35,037 --> 00:04:36,810
perform without those boundaries.

75
00:04:36,892 --> 00:04:41,121
The model has no general authority
to change retail systems.

76
00:04:41,373 --> 00:04:45,233
Next, let's look at how an incident
moves through this architecture

77
00:04:46,233 --> 00:04:47,899
A controlled incident lifecycle.

78
00:04:48,335 --> 00:04:52,785
The lifecycle begins with detection
of a persistent failure pattern.

79
00:04:52,948 --> 00:04:56,803
We gather enough evidence to establish
that the problem needs attention.

80
00:04:57,137 --> 00:04:59,009
The agent then recommends a response.

81
00:04:59,521 --> 00:05:03,454
It explains a candidate cause,
identifies the affected scope,

82
00:05:03,511 --> 00:05:05,140
and gives supporting observations.

83
00:05:05,734 --> 00:05:08,822
Containment follows only
if the policy allows it.

84
00:05:08,882 --> 00:05:12,705
In this example, that could
mean temporarily disabling new

85
00:05:12,725 --> 00:05:14,253
orders for the affected store.

86
00:05:14,621 --> 00:05:19,220
After the underlying problem has
been addressed, an author-- an

87
00:05:19,230 --> 00:05:21,883
authorized person initiates recovery.

88
00:05:22,113 --> 00:05:25,184
The workflow runs the required
checks and permits reopening

89
00:05:25,216 --> 00:05:27,178
only when those checks pass.

90
00:05:27,459 --> 00:05:31,213
These stages should have explicit
states and recorded outcomes.

91
00:05:31,481 --> 00:05:34,423
A failed check keeps the
incident open for the work.

92
00:05:34,714 --> 00:05:38,530
A repeated request must not
skip a stage or repeat an action

93
00:05:38,842 --> 00:05:40,054
that has already completed.

94
00:05:40,687 --> 00:05:43,650
The sentence at the bottom
captures the responsibility split.

95
00:05:44,159 --> 00:05:45,229
AI advises.

96
00:05:46,044 --> 00:05:47,242
Policy authorizes.

97
00:05:47,437 --> 00:05:48,758
Human controlled reopening.

98
00:05:48,884 --> 00:05:53,897
Let's examine detection first,
because every latter decision depends

99
00:05:53,898 --> 00:05:55,774
on the quality of that evidence

100
00:05:56,774 --> 00:06:00,076
Detecting persistent
failures, not isolated noise.

101
00:06:00,595 --> 00:06:04,138
A single timeout can have
several explanations.

102
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:08,732
Before taking a disruptive action,
the design needs evidence of sustained

103
00:06:08,816 --> 00:06:11,891
degradation and its business impact.

104
00:06:12,447 --> 00:06:16,766
Signal fusion means looking at
transaction failures alongside

105
00:06:17,172 --> 00:06:22,527
timeouts, dependency health, event
stream status, and store context.

106
00:06:22,789 --> 00:06:25,719
We also need the time window
and transaction volume.

107
00:06:27,217 --> 00:06:32,204
A failure count has limited meaning
without knowing how much traffic occurred.

108
00:06:32,722 --> 00:06:35,297
Context helps identify the scope.

109
00:06:35,457 --> 00:06:40,458
If one store is affected while
comparable stores are healthy, we

110
00:06:40,470 --> 00:06:42,129
investigate local dependencies.

111
00:06:42,818 --> 00:06:48,558
If failures appear across stores using
the same integration, that suggests

112
00:06:48,635 --> 00:06:51,780
a dependency deserves attention.

113
00:06:52,243 --> 00:06:56,040
These are hypotheses that
the evidence must support.

114
00:06:57,553 --> 00:06:58,701
Freshness also matters.

115
00:06:58,993 --> 00:07:03,206
Delayed events should not be treated as
current failures without checking their

116
00:07:03,248 --> 00:07:05,684
timestamps and the present incident state.

117
00:07:06,047 --> 00:07:11,000
I would define persistence thresholds
and observation window as explicit

118
00:07:11,016 --> 00:07:14,786
configuration, then validate them
against representative traffic.

119
00:07:14,853 --> 00:07:17,373
The raw evidence remains
available for review.

120
00:07:17,778 --> 00:07:20,923
With that foundation, the agent
can help interpret the incident

121
00:07:21,923 --> 00:07:25,448
AI-assisted diagnosis
with bounded authority.

122
00:07:25,835 --> 00:07:29,982
The agent receives recent failures,
dependency changes, affected stores,

123
00:07:30,023 --> 00:07:32,320
and a relevant incident history.

124
00:07:32,633 --> 00:07:38,383
Its job is to turn those observations
into a useful assessment.

125
00:07:38,393 --> 00:07:44,491
For example, it might say payment
times out, incre-increased for the

126
00:07:44,493 --> 00:07:49,217
store after a dependency change,
while inventory checks remain healthy.

127
00:07:49,841 --> 00:07:53,422
A payment integration issue is a
candidate cause, but the evidence

128
00:07:53,423 --> 00:07:55,432
does not yet confirm the root cause.

129
00:07:55,745 --> 00:08:00,224
A useful recommendation identifies
the supporting observation, missing

130
00:08:00,224 --> 00:08:02,261
information, and a suggested action.

131
00:08:02,549 --> 00:08:05,943
An interpre-- An operator
should be able to trace that

132
00:08:05,969 --> 00:08:08,184
explanation back to actual events.

133
00:08:08,660 --> 00:08:11,890
The guardrail is enforced
outside the model.

134
00:08:11,948 --> 00:08:15,744
The agent does not receive
unrestricted credential to modify

135
00:08:15,744 --> 00:08:17,936
payments, inventory, or store status.

136
00:08:18,641 --> 00:08:23,453
Any proposed action goes through the
policy engine and a constrained workflow.

137
00:08:23,779 --> 00:08:27,068
A confident explanation still
needs evidence and authorization.

138
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:31,171
If the assessment is incomplete
or inconsistent, the system

139
00:08:31,184 --> 00:08:32,950
should request review.

140
00:08:33,148 --> 00:08:36,182
That brings us to the
conditions for safe containment

141
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:39,585
Policy-driven temporary store disablement.

142
00:08:40,275 --> 00:08:45,854
In this example, store disablement means
temporarily stopping the affected store

143
00:08:45,856 --> 00:08:47,990
from accepting the new digital orders.

144
00:08:48,339 --> 00:08:51,017
The actions has a defined business scope.

145
00:08:51,313 --> 00:08:54,719
Before applying it, the policy
checks that the incident is active

146
00:08:55,214 --> 00:08:56,412
and the evidence is correct.

147
00:08:56,912 --> 00:09:01,987
It verifies that both the store and the
requested action are explicitly permitted.

148
00:09:02,354 --> 00:09:04,894
The workflow also checks
for duplicate requests.

149
00:09:04,961 --> 00:09:09,137
If the same incident event arrives
again, it should not trigger another

150
00:09:09,157 --> 00:09:10,546
independent disablement action.

151
00:09:11,111 --> 00:09:15,768
Customer-facing availability
must stay consistent with the

152
00:09:16,013 --> 00:09:17,585
actual order acceptance state.

153
00:09:17,905 --> 00:09:23,419
If one update succeeds and another
fails, the workflow needs to record

154
00:09:23,419 --> 00:09:26,590
that partial result, reconcile
it, and alert the operator.

155
00:09:27,016 --> 00:09:31,235
Finally, the action and its
rationale must be recorded durably.

156
00:09:31,602 --> 00:09:35,858
We need to know what was requested, what
was allowed, and what actually happened.

157
00:09:36,219 --> 00:09:38,605
Existing transactions
still require attention.

158
00:09:38,922 --> 00:09:42,847
Disabling new orders does not
resolve a depending pay-- a pending

159
00:09:42,858 --> 00:09:45,885
payment or an inventory reservation.

160
00:09:46,141 --> 00:09:51,037
It limits new exposure while
investigation and remediation continue.

161
00:09:51,524 --> 00:09:55,608
Recovery therefore needs its own
decision and validation process

162
00:09:56,608 --> 00:09:58,535
Recovery remains human-initiated.

163
00:09:58,987 --> 00:10:03,431
An authorized operator initiates the
recovery process after reviewing the

164
00:10:03,444 --> 00:10:07,419
incident, the remediation status,
and the scope proposed for reopening.

165
00:10:07,889 --> 00:10:12,350
That decision is enforced through
role-based access and explicit

166
00:10:12,694 --> 00:10:14,572
approval linked to the incident.

167
00:10:14,805 --> 00:10:20,862
The workflow records who initiated it
and which stores the request covers.

168
00:10:21,096 --> 00:10:25,904
Repeated clicks, re-retried requests,
or old events must not create a

169
00:10:25,907 --> 00:10:30,020
sec-second recovery process or
bypass an incomplete validation step.

170
00:10:30,716 --> 00:10:34,892
The system checks the current workflow
state before accepting a transition.

171
00:10:35,628 --> 00:10:38,086
There is a practical reason
for this human checkpoint.

172
00:10:38,405 --> 00:10:45,214
Once new orders stop, transaction
failures may not stop appearing as well.

173
00:10:45,648 --> 00:10:50,125
A quiet dashboard can simply mean
there is no traffic left to fail.

174
00:10:50,831 --> 00:10:53,916
The operator initiates recovery,
and the system enforces the

175
00:10:53,916 --> 00:10:55,079
validation requirements.

176
00:10:55,402 --> 00:10:58,798
If a check fails, the store
remains contained, and the

177
00:10:58,820 --> 00:11:00,909
operator receives the result.

178
00:11:01,047 --> 00:11:04,277
The next slide defines the
evidence required for reopening

179
00:11:05,277 --> 00:11:06,503
Validate before reopening.

180
00:11:07,594 --> 00:11:10,147
The recovery gate checks four areas.

181
00:11:10,324 --> 00:11:11,862
First is a dependency health.

182
00:11:12,739 --> 00:11:18,314
The integrations required by the affected
transaction path must respond as expected.

183
00:11:18,399 --> 00:11:21,566
A basic process health check
alone cannot establish that.

184
00:11:22,276 --> 00:11:23,766
Second is the transaction path.

185
00:11:24,094 --> 00:11:28,477
We need a representative flow that
describes the relevant steps, including

186
00:11:28,478 --> 00:11:30,406
the expected downstream result.

187
00:11:31,011 --> 00:11:35,378
Any test that touches business
system needs an approved method

188
00:11:35,399 --> 00:11:36,805
and controlled side effects.

189
00:11:37,352 --> 00:11:39,222
Third is state consistency.

190
00:11:40,571 --> 00:11:43,531
Pending and failed work
must be reconciled.

191
00:11:43,576 --> 00:11:47,540
For example, a payment timeout
does not tell us whether the

192
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:50,081
provider processed the payment.

193
00:11:50,591 --> 00:11:56,422
We need to establish the actual outcome
before deciding what happens to the

194
00:11:56,428 --> 00:11:58,347
order or attempting another payment.

195
00:11:58,804 --> 00:12:00,582
Fourth is an observation window.

196
00:12:01,144 --> 00:12:05,565
A brief successful response may not
demonstrate sustained stability.

197
00:12:06,024 --> 00:12:09,651
The required duration and success
criteria should be defined for

198
00:12:09,653 --> 00:12:11,068
the workflow and the incident.

199
00:12:11,876 --> 00:12:15,798
Reopening is permitted only
after the required checks pass.

200
00:12:15,841 --> 00:12:20,388
If evidence is missing, a dependency
fails again, or reconciliation is

201
00:12:20,417 --> 00:12:25,348
incomplete, the workflow reports that
result and keeps the store contained.

202
00:12:25,589 --> 00:12:29,743
Let's apply this process to
one illustrative incident

203
00:12:30,743 --> 00:12:33,425
Representative incident
transaction failures.

204
00:12:33,768 --> 00:12:37,440
Consider a store that is
accepting digital orders normally.

205
00:12:37,900 --> 00:12:41,793
We then observe a sustained
cluster of checkout failures

206
00:12:42,168 --> 00:12:44,109
associated with payment timeouts.

207
00:12:44,337 --> 00:12:49,118
The detection layer correlates those
failures with dependency signals

208
00:12:49,375 --> 00:12:51,132
and identifies the affected store.

209
00:12:51,445 --> 00:12:54,477
The agent reviews the evidence
and proposes a payment dependency

210
00:12:54,484 --> 00:12:56,182
issue as a likely explanation.

211
00:12:56,466 --> 00:12:58,797
It also states what remained uncertain.

212
00:12:59,046 --> 00:13:02,187
The policy engine checks the
current incident, permitted

213
00:13:02,187 --> 00:13:04,888
scope, and containment conditions.

214
00:13:05,199 --> 00:13:09,039
If those checks pass, the workflow
temporarily disables new order acceptance

215
00:13:09,052 --> 00:13:13,027
for that store, updates availability,
and notifies the responsible team.

216
00:13:13,344 --> 00:13:16,268
The team investigates and
addresses the dependency problem.

217
00:13:16,666 --> 00:13:19,300
An authorized operator then
initiates the recovery.

218
00:13:19,574 --> 00:13:22,571
The workflow checks the integration,
runs an approved representative

219
00:13:22,576 --> 00:13:27,234
transaction, and reconciles orders
whose payment outcomes were uncertain.

220
00:13:27,588 --> 00:13:31,994
After the configured observation
window passes with the required checks

221
00:13:31,998 --> 00:13:33,832
successful, the store can reopen.

222
00:13:33,890 --> 00:13:38,595
If a check fails, containment
continues, the operator sees why.

223
00:13:40,003 --> 00:13:43,273
This example illustrates the
intended behavior of the design.

224
00:13:43,325 --> 00:13:45,847
Each step produces evidence
for the next decision.

225
00:13:46,149 --> 00:13:48,280
An incomplete step remains visible.

226
00:13:48,710 --> 00:13:51,513
Supporting that behavior requires
durable coordination, which

227
00:13:51,514 --> 00:13:52,646
is the focus of the next slide

228
00:13:53,646 --> 00:13:55,457
Event streaming and durable coordination.

229
00:13:55,537 --> 00:14:00,227
The event backbone carries transaction
outcomes, incident transactions, action

230
00:14:00,245 --> 00:14:01,668
requests, and validation results.

231
00:14:02,065 --> 00:14:07,018
These events need enough identity
and context to connect them to right

232
00:14:07,019 --> 00:14:08,863
store, transaction, and incident.

233
00:14:09,389 --> 00:14:15,968
The workflow pushes its current state,
action status, retries, and checkpoints.

234
00:14:16,525 --> 00:14:20,835
If a worker restarts, it should be
able to determine which steps completed

235
00:14:20,862 --> 00:14:22,272
and which still need attention.

236
00:14:22,589 --> 00:14:27,864
Imagine that a disablement request
succeeds, but its response is lost.

237
00:14:28,623 --> 00:14:32,620
The workflow may retry because
it cannot confirm the outcome.

238
00:14:33,566 --> 00:14:37,714
An idempotent command allow
that retry without creating

239
00:14:37,714 --> 00:14:39,633
an additional business effect.

240
00:14:39,745 --> 00:14:44,054
It can recognize the same request and
return or reconcile the existing result.

241
00:14:44,517 --> 00:14:47,242
Partial failures also
need explicit handling.

242
00:14:47,652 --> 00:14:51,198
The store state might change while
the customer-facing update fails.

243
00:14:51,799 --> 00:14:54,367
We must retain both
outcomes and reconcile them.

244
00:14:54,762 --> 00:14:59,308
Durable coordination makes the recovery
process itself resilient to interruptions.

245
00:14:59,363 --> 00:15:02,998
It also gives operators a
trustworthy view of progress.

246
00:15:03,355 --> 00:15:06,885
Those records and actions
need clear security boundaries

247
00:15:07,885 --> 00:15:10,015
Security, governance, and auditability.

248
00:15:10,436 --> 00:15:12,539
The first boundary is least privilege.

249
00:15:13,366 --> 00:15:18,761
Observation, recommendation, containment,
and reopening have separate permissions.

250
00:15:19,036 --> 00:15:22,814
Access to incident evidence
should not automatically grant the

251
00:15:22,835 --> 00:15:25,246
ability to change store status.

252
00:15:25,439 --> 00:15:27,932
The second boundary is
deterministic policy.

253
00:15:28,309 --> 00:15:32,721
The system evaluates action scope
and approval outside the model.

254
00:15:33,304 --> 00:15:37,655
Instructions in a prompt are not a
substitute for authorized checks at

255
00:15:37,655 --> 00:15:39,401
the point where an action executes.

256
00:15:39,717 --> 00:15:41,426
The third requirement is traceability.

257
00:15:41,718 --> 00:15:45,972
The incident record should connect the
evidence, the recommendation, the policy

258
00:15:45,977 --> 00:15:50,594
decision, the operator identity when
applicable, and the actual action outcome.

259
00:15:50,988 --> 00:15:56,940
Finally, the design needs
defined behavior when information

260
00:15:56,941 --> 00:15:58,134
is missing or conflicting.

261
00:15:58,464 --> 00:16:02,656
If the system cannot establish
that a state-changing action is

262
00:16:02,685 --> 00:16:07,110
authorized, it should preserve the
current state and escalate for review.

263
00:16:07,462 --> 00:16:13,284
Uncertainty alone should not trigger a
broad shutdown or an unverified reopening.

264
00:16:13,705 --> 00:16:18,178
These controls need to hold during
failures as well as normal operation.

265
00:16:18,619 --> 00:16:22,348
That is where deliberate failure
testing becomes essential

266
00:16:23,348 --> 00:16:25,773
Testing the design and
planning what comes next.

267
00:16:26,312 --> 00:16:30,203
For a chaos engineering experiment, I
would begin with an explicit hypothesis.

268
00:16:30,470 --> 00:16:35,294
For example, a sustained dependency
failure affecting one test store should

269
00:16:35,326 --> 00:16:37,635
trigger only the permitted containment.

270
00:16:37,711 --> 00:16:41,797
New order acceptance must remain
disabled until an authorized

271
00:16:41,828 --> 00:16:46,034
operator initiates recovery and
every required validation passes.

272
00:16:46,643 --> 00:16:50,548
I would define the steady-state
transaction behavior, limit the

273
00:16:50,548 --> 00:16:55,359
experiment scope, and set stop
conditions before injecting failures.

274
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:58,503
Initial experiments should run
in a controlled environment.

275
00:16:58,854 --> 00:17:01,203
Then I would exercise
the cases on the slide.

276
00:17:01,760 --> 00:17:06,621
A transient error should not meet a
policy that requires sustained failures.

277
00:17:06,714 --> 00:17:10,201
Duplicate events should not
repeat business actions.

278
00:17:10,359 --> 00:17:14,694
Stale signals should not override
the current incident state.

279
00:17:15,185 --> 00:17:18,096
Permission denial should
prevent execution.

280
00:17:18,424 --> 00:17:23,411
I would also interrupt disablement
partway through, restart the workflow,

281
00:17:23,884 --> 00:17:29,164
and attempt reopening while a dependency
or reconciliation check is still failing.

282
00:17:29,585 --> 00:17:34,078
We need to observe whether partial
results remain visible and whether

283
00:17:34,107 --> 00:17:35,397
the workflow resumes correctly.

284
00:17:35,872 --> 00:17:41,487
I would measure unnecessary
containment, time to contain duplicate

285
00:17:41,618 --> 00:17:44,851
side effects, incomplete recovery
checks, and audit completeness.

286
00:17:45,264 --> 00:17:51,162
For this proposed architecture, those
measures would esta-- help establish

287
00:17:51,171 --> 00:17:54,381
whether the control work as intended.

288
00:17:55,285 --> 00:17:58,927
Predictive detection, cross-store
correlation, and digital twins

289
00:17:58,973 --> 00:18:00,647
are possible future extensions.

290
00:18:00,951 --> 00:18:05,037
I would consider them after
the core controls demonstrate

291
00:18:05,492 --> 00:18:07,110
reliable behavior under failure

292
00:18:08,110 --> 00:18:08,404
Thank you.

293
00:18:08,841 --> 00:18:13,647
The central idea is that retail
recovery needs evidence about

294
00:18:13,662 --> 00:18:15,413
the complete transaction journey.

295
00:18:15,524 --> 00:18:20,197
Agentic AI can help interpret that
evidence and recommend a response.

296
00:18:20,264 --> 00:18:23,251
Accruise policy limits containment.

297
00:18:23,317 --> 00:18:28,809
An authorized person initiates reopening,
and the validation establishes whether

298
00:18:28,826 --> 00:18:30,750
the business flow is ready to resume.

299
00:18:31,077 --> 00:18:34,974
Chaos engineering gives us a way
to test those responsibilities when

300
00:18:34,984 --> 00:18:39,200
dependencies fail, events repeat,
and workflows are interrupted.

301
00:18:39,517 --> 00:18:44,331
As the closing slide says, engineering
resilience means recovering with

302
00:18:44,369 --> 00:18:46,451
evidence, policy, and control.

303
00:18:46,780 --> 00:18:47,582
Thank you for watching.

304
00:18:47,612 --> 00:18:51,801
I'm Kishore Chandra Rajapudi, and I look
forward to your questions and discussion
